---
import Permalink from './Permalink.astro';
import Clip from './Clip.astro';
import { docs, links, standards, withBase } from '../data/content';
---
<section id="developers" aria-labelledby="dev-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">For developers</span>
      <div class="anchored"><h2 id="dev-title">Built on an SDK you can use.</h2><Permalink to="developers" label="For developers" /></div>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything SoundCharts does is in <strong>EncDotNet.S100</strong>, a managed .NET library for reading,
        portraying, rendering and validating S-100 data. It has no native dependencies and no commercial S-52 assets.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="dev-grid">
      <div id="developers-sdk" class="dev">
        <span class="eyebrow">Library</span>
        <div class="anchored"><h3>Three lines to a PNG.</h3><Permalink to="developers-sdk" label="The .NET library" /></div>
        <p>Open any supported dataset, and the bundled feature and portrayal catalogues take care of the rest.</p>
        <pre><span class="k">using var</span> ds = S100Dataset.Open(<span class="s">"chart.000"</span>);
<span class="k">using var</span> r  = <span class="k">new</span> PngS100DatasetRenderer();
<span class="k">byte</span>[] png = <span class="k">await</span> r.RenderAsync(ds);</pre>
        <div class="links">
          <a href={withBase(docs.gettingStarted)}>Get started</a>
          <a href={links.nuget}>NuGet</a>
          <a href={withBase(docs.api)}>API reference</a>
          <a href={withBase(docs.s57)}>S-57 libraries</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="developers-cli" class="dev">
        <span class="eyebrow">Command line</span>
        <div class="anchored"><h3>Render and serve from the shell.</h3><Permalink to="developers-cli" label="The command line" /></div>
        <p>
          The self-contained <code>s100</code> tool renders, inspects and validates data, checks routes and predicts
          tides. It serves a folder of charts as map tiles for web maps and GIS, with WMTS and WMS. It needs no .NET
          install, and there's a Docker image too.
        </p>
        <pre><span class="c"># a folder of charts, as tiles</span>
docker run -p 8200:8200 \
  -v ./charts:/data:ro \
  ghcr.io/philliphoff/s100 \
  tiles serve /data --host 0.0.0.0</pre>
        <div class="links">
          <a href={withBase(docs.cli)}>CLI guide</a>
          <a href={withBase(docs.serveTiles)}>Serve tiles</a>
          <a href={withBase(docs.docker)}>Docker</a>
          <a href={links.releases}>Download</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="developers-mcp" class="dev">
        <span class="eyebrow">MCP</span>
        <div class="anchored"><h3>Let agents read the chart.</h3><Permalink to="developers-mcp" label="The MCP server" /></div>
        <p>
          An optional MCP server lets AI agents query features, sample depths and currents, plan routes and drive the
          viewer. <code>s100 mcp serve</code> runs the same tools without the app, locally or over HTTP.
        </p>
        <Clip id="D2" alt="An AI agent plans a route in SoundCharts over MCP, finds a shoal with S-102 depths and moves two waypoints" title="Agent planning a route" detail="Transcript next to the viewer" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 330px" />
        <div class="links"><a href={withBase(docs.mcp)}>MCP server docs</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="standards" class="standards-block" style="margin-top: 72px">
      <div class="anchored"><h3 style="font-size: 26px; margin-bottom: 18px">Supported standards</h3><Permalink to="standards" label="Supported standards" /></div>
      <div class="standards">
        {standards.map(([spec, subject]) => <div class="std"><b>{spec}</b><span>{subject}</span></div>)}
      </div>
      <p class="std-note">
        Each standard comes with a reader and portrayal, and validation rule packs where the spec defines them.
        <a href={links.standards}>Full details →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
